\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Let $(X,\mathcal X,\mu)$ be a probability space. For each positive integer $q$, let $T_q$ be an invertible measure-preserving transformation, with $T_1=\operatorname{id}$ and $T_mT_n=T_{mn}$ for all $m,n\ge1$. This is a system with multiplicative structure. The harmless convention $T_0=\operatorname{id}$ is also used in the source, although no zero index occurs below. For a measurable set $A$, $T_qA$ denotes its image.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every such system and every $A\in\mathcal X$ with $\mu(A)>0$, are there positive integers $m>n$ such that \[\mu\bigl(T_{2mn}A\cap T_{(m-n)(m+n)}A\bigr)>0?\] \href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1103.3808v3}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Does every positive-measure set recur under two multiplicative transformations whose indices form the legs of a Pythagorean triple?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 4800034 (AMR-047-0034). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] N. Frantzikinakis, Some open problems on multiple ergodic averages (2016), Section 8, multiplicative-system definition and full Problem 34, p.36. \url{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1103.3808v3}.
\end{description}
